sections.s2_technical:
GET retrieves a representation of a resource and must be safe, meaning it never changes server state, and idempotent, meaning calling it many times has the same effect as calling it once. For example `GET /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001` returns Rajesh's account and changes nothing. GET requests can be cached.

POST creates a new resource under a collection URL, or triggers a process. It is neither safe nor idempotent — two identical POSTs usually create two resources. For example `POST /api/v1/transfers` with a transfer body creates a brand new transfer and typically returns `201 Created` with a `Location` header pointing to the new resource.

```java
@PostMapping("/transfers")
public ResponseEntity<TransferResponse> create(@Valid @RequestBody TransferRequest r) {
    TransferResponse resp = service.create(r);
    return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/v1/transfers/" + resp.id())).body(resp);
}
```

PUT fully replaces the resource at a known URL, or creates it if the client controls the ID. It is idempotent: sending the same full representation twice leaves the resource in the same final state. For example `PUT /api/v1/beneficiaries/BEN-77` with a complete beneficiary body replaces that beneficiary entirely. If you omit a field, PUT semantics say that field becomes null or default, because PUT means "this is the whole thing now."

PATCH applies a partial modification. You send only the fields you want to change. It is not guaranteed idempotent in general, though many implementations make it so. For example `PATCH /api/v1/beneficiaries/BEN-77` with `{"nickname":"Landlord"}` changes only the nickname and leaves every other field untouched.

DELETE removes the resource. It is idempotent: deleting an already-deleted resource still leaves it deleted, so the end state is the same. A common pattern returns `204 No Content` on success. For example `DELETE /api/v1/beneficiaries/BEN-77` removes Priya's saved landlord beneficiary.

HEAD behaves exactly like GET but the server returns only the status line and headers, with no body. It is used to check existence, size via `Content-Length`, or freshness via `ETag` or `Last-Modified` without downloading the payload. For example `HEAD /api/v1/statements/2026-09` lets the app know a statement exists and how big it is before downloading.

OPTIONS asks the server which methods are allowed on a resource. The response carries an `Allow` header like `Allow: GET, PUT, DELETE`. It is also the mechanism behind CORS preflight requests from browsers. For example a browser sends `OPTIONS /api/v1/transfers` before a cross-origin POST to check it is permitted.

A senior nuance: the properties of safe and idempotent are contracts the whole ecosystem depends on. A proxy may freely retry a GET or PUT after a network glitch because repeating them is harmless, but it must not blindly retry a POST, because that could create a duplicate transfer and move Rajesh's money twice. Designing your verbs correctly is therefore not cosmetic — it directly affects correctness under retries.

